{-
   External iterators - the handle a `fn f(...) -> iter(T)` call builds, and the operations the
   compiler emits around one.

   `doc/inflight/Analysis-External-Iterators.md` §2.1 declares an external iterator by its *result
   type*. Everything the compiler does with one is a call of a function below, claimed by a row of
   `compiler/intrinsic/known.def`: a call site hands `Iter.new` the arguments and an entry, a `for`
   asks `Iter.advance` and takes the value with `Iter.take`, a `yield` is `IterBlock.handOver`, and
   the entry the compiler synthesizes opens with `IterBlock.begin` and ends with `IterBlock.finish`.
   `compiler/resolve_mir/resolve_iter.cpp` is the other half.

   Every function is under its type's namespace. `Fiber` is imported whole, and `next`, `take` or
   `finish` at the top level of it would claim names every importing program would want.

   § Two records

   `Iter(y, a)` is what a consumer holds, and it is one pointer. What it points at is `IterBlock`,
   written at the **top of the producer's own stack mapping**, above the stack - storage whose address
   does not change for as long as the stack exists, which is exactly as long as the producer can run.
   So the handle may move like any other value: the producer never knew where it was. The top rather
   than the bottom because the bottom is where an overrun arrives, and it has to find the guard page
   there rather than the block.

   `a` is the tuple the call captured, and **`captured` is the last field on purpose**. A producer is
   compiled against `IterBlock(y, {})`, because its signature is settled before any call decides
   what the tuple holds; every field it reads comes first, so their offsets do not depend on `a`.
   Only `IterBlock.begin` and the handle's teardown read `captured`, and both see the real type.

   § Cancellation

   Dropping a handle whose producer is suspended **resumes it with `kCancel`** - §3.5's synchronous
   cancellation. `IterBlock.handOver` answers that, the compiler makes the `yield` a `return`, and the
   producer's teardown runs on its own stack before the handle gives the stack back. A consumer that
   leaves a `for` early therefore drops what the producer owned exactly once, and so does one that
   never asked for anything: the arguments are still in `captured`, and the teardown drops them.

   § What is not checked

   What a producer borrowed from the consumer's frame is checked by the compiler rather than here:
   the call is of a synthesized `f$new` whose result borrows every lent argument, so the handle's
   type says nothing about it. What is not checked is the stack's size, which is a constant - §4.5's
   bound is not built. A producer that overruns it faults on the guard page `guardedStack` puts under
   it, rather than writing over whatever is mapped next to it.
-}

import Native

-- What a switch carries. The two directions are separate words because they are separate
-- questions: what the consumer wants, and what the producer did. Prefixed because `Cursor` beside
-- this file is the same module and already names its own pair.
--
-- A word-sized value travels as the producer's word itself (`IterBlock.inWord`), so the consumer
-- reads whether the producer finished from the fiber's status rather than from the word.
let kIterAdvance = 1 :: Size
let kIterCancel = 2 :: Size
let kIterYielded = 1 :: Size
let kIterFinished = 0 :: Size

-- Every producer's stack - a number the library picks until §4.5 computes one.
let kIterStackBytes = 65536 :: Size

{-
   The producer's state, at the top of its stack mapping.

   `@builtin(iterBlock)`. Field order is part of the contract - see the file comment - so
   `captured` stays last, and `@layout(c)` is what makes last mean last. The default layout orders
   fields by size, so a 24-byte `Maybe({StringView})` was placed *first* and the producer, reading the
   block at `IterBlock(y, {})`, switched to a stack pointer that was the view's bytes.
-}
@builtin(iterBlock)
@layout(c)
pub data IterBlock(y, a) {
    fiber: Fiber,

    -- The one value in flight: filled by `IterBlock.handOver`, emptied by `Iter.take`. Unused for a
    -- value that travels in `word` instead.
    slot: Maybe(y),

    -- The word the producer's last switch answered the consumer with - the value itself where
    -- `IterBlock.inWord` holds. A register's worth of bytes rather than a `Maybe(y)` exchange, which
    -- cost the x64 step a blocked store-to-load forward per value (the switch-cost note in
    -- Analysis-External-Iterators.md).
    word: Size,

    stack: %U8,
    stackBytes: Size,

    -- What a *lending* producer is showing its consumer: the address of a value on the producer's
    -- own stack, valid while the producer is suspended at the `yield` that set it, and null
    -- otherwise. See `LentIter`.
    lent: %y,

    -- The arguments, until the entry takes them - and after a producer that never ran, until the
    -- handle's teardown does.
    captured: Maybe(a)
}

-- A consumer's handle. Null where the stack could not be mapped, which is an iterator with nothing
-- in it.
@builtin(iterState)
pub data Iter(y, a) {
    block: %IterBlock(y, a)
}

{-
   The handle of a producer that **lends** what it yields - `-> iter(T)` where `T` has a teardown -
   Analysis-External-Iterators.md §3.3.

   A second record rather than a flag on `Iter`, because the difference is one a *type* has to carry:
   a consumer holding a handle decides from its type whether it takes a value or borrows one, and a
   `for` over a local has nothing else to read. The block and the stack are the same.

   What makes a lent value sound is the signature of `LentIter.current` and nothing in its body. Its
   result borrows `self`, so the loan checker keeps the handle borrowed for as long as the yielded
   value is used - and advancing, cancelling or dropping the handle all need it whole, so none of
   them can happen while the value is still live. That is §3.3's "the consumer cannot advance the
   iterator while that borrow is live", stated as the ordinary rule for a `'T` result.
-}
@builtin(iterLentState)
pub data LentIter(y, a) {
    block: %IterBlock(y, a)
}

{-
   A handle, with the producer prepared and nothing run - §4.2's creation.

   `entry` is the function the compiler synthesized for this producer and this tuple, and it is
   handed the block at `IterBlock(y, {})`, which is the type the producer's own parameter has.
-}
@builtin(iterNew)
pub fn (y, a) Iter.new(->captured: a, entry: (%IterBlock(y, {})) -> {}) -> Iter(y, a) =
    Iter {block: IterBlock.create(captured, entry)}

-- The same, for a producer that lends.
@builtin(iterLentNew)
pub fn (y, a) LentIter.new(->captured: a, entry: (%IterBlock(y, {})) -> {}) -> LentIter(y, a) =
    LentIter {block: IterBlock.create(captured, entry)}

-- The block both handles point at, or null where the stack could not be mapped.
fn (y, a) IterBlock.create(->captured: a, entry: (%IterBlock(y, {})) -> {}) -> %IterBlock(y, a):
    let stack = guardedStack(kIterStackBytes)
    if isNull(stack) then return null()

    -- The block at the top of the mapping and the producer's stack under it, down to the guard. At
    -- the top because a stack overruns downwards: a block at the bottom would be the first thing an
    -- overrun wrote over, before it ever reached the guard. Aligned to 64, which is as strongly as
    -- any captured value can ask.
    let shape = traits() :: TypeTraits(IterBlock(y, a))
    let end = bitcast(stack + kIterStackBytes) :: Size
    let at = (end - shape.size) `and` not(63 :: Size)
    let bottom = bitcast(stack + stackGuardBytes()) :: Size

    let block = bitcast(at) :: %IterBlock(y, a)
    let context = bitcast(at) :: %IterBlock(y, {})
    let base = bitcast(bottom) :: %U8
    let prepared = newFiber(base, at - bottom, context, entry)

    -- Ascribed, because the shipping stage reads this file too - every fixture that imports `Fiber`
    -- compiles it - and it does not take a record literal's arguments from the pointer it is stored
    -- through.
    let made = IterBlock {
        fiber: prepared,
        slot: Nothing :: Maybe(y),
        stack: stack,
        stackBytes: kIterStackBytes,
        word: 0,
        lent: null(),
        captured: Just(captured)
    } :: IterBlock(y, a)

    store(block, made)

    return block

-- The lending producer, run to its next `yield` - `Iter.advance`, for the other handle.
@builtin(iterLentNext)
pub fn (y, a) LentIter.advance(&self: LentIter(y, a)) -> Bool = IterBlock.advance(self.block)

{-
   The value the last `LentIter.advance` is showing, borrowed from the handle.

   Only after an `LentIter.advance` that answered `True`, which is the one order the compiler emits;
   anything else stops the program rather than reading a null. The result borrows `self`, which is
   what keeps the producer from being resumed underneath it - see `LentIter`.
-}
@builtin(iterCurrent)
pub fn (y, a) LentIter.current(self: source'LentIter(y, a)) -> source'y:
    let at = LentIter.shown(self)
    if isNull(at) then abortProcess()

    return borrow(at)

-- The address the producer is showing, as a pointer into `self`'s storage - `String.units`' idiom,
-- which is what lets `current` borrow through it: a pointer read out of the block names no origin
-- by itself, and this signature is where one is stated.
fn (y, a) LentIter.shown(self: source'LentIter(y, a)) -> %source'y = borrow(self.block).lent

{-
   §2.4's manual step for a lending producer. The value borrows the handle exclusively, so the next
   `next` is refused until it is dead - §3.3's `blocks.next()` "rejected while `first` is still
   live".
-}
pub fn (y, a) LentIter.next(&self: 'LentIter(y, a)) -> Maybe('y):
    if !LentIter.advance(self) then return Nothing
    return Just(LentIter.current(self))

{-
   `yield value` inside a lending producer: `value` is shown to the consumer until it resumes, and it
   stays in this frame the whole time. `True` where the consumer answered by cancelling.

   An address and not a borrow, for the reason `suspendOn` takes one: the consumer's loan is checked
   on the consumer's side, against the handle, and this frame is not running while it exists.
-}
@builtin(iterLend)
pub fn (y) IterBlock.lend(block: %IterBlock(y, {}), value: %y) -> Bool:
    let &me = borrowMut(block)
    me.lent = value

    let cancelled = suspendOn(addressOf(me.fiber), kIterYielded) == kIterCancel
    me.lent = null()

    return cancelled

instance (y, a) Drop(LentIter(y, a)):
    fn drop(->value: LentIter(y, a)):
        IterBlock.close(value.block)

-- The producer, run to its next hand-over. `False` where it finished, this time or earlier.
@builtin(iterNext)
pub fn (y, a) Iter.advance(&self: Iter(y, a)) -> Bool = IterBlock.advance(self.block)

{-
   Both handles' step: the block they point at is the same, and so is what stepping it means.

   The switch is written out rather than called through `resume`, which would test the status a
   second time. Whether the producer finished is the fiber's status after the switch - `finishOn`
   sets it - so that the word coming back is free to be the value (`IterBlock.handOver`).
-}
fn (y, a) IterBlock.advance(block: %IterBlock(y, a)) -> Bool:
    if isNull(block) then return False

    let &me = borrowMut(block)
    if me.fiber.status == FiberStatus.Finished then return False

    me.fiber.status = FiberStatus.Suspended
    me.word = stackSwitch(me.fiber.child, addressOf(me.fiber.parent), kIterAdvance)

    return me.fiber.status == FiberStatus.Suspended

{-
   Whether a `y` travels in the switch's transfer word rather than in the slot: a trivially copyable
   value no larger than the word. Its bytes are written into a zeroed word and read back out of
   `IterBlock.word`, so a narrower value is its low bytes on both sides.

   A fact about the type, so every instantiation answers a constant and the other path is dead.
-}
@inline(always)
fn (y, a) IterBlock.inWord(block: %IterBlock(y, a)) -> Bool =
    (traits() :: TypeTraits(y)).trivialCopy && (traits() :: TypeTraits(y)).size <= (traits() :: TypeTraits(Size)).size

{-
   The value the last `Iter.advance` produced, taken out of the slot.

   Only after an `Iter.advance` that answered `True`, which is the one order the compiler emits. An
   empty slot stops the program - see `unreachableValue`.
-}
@builtin(iterTake)
pub fn (y, a) Iter.take(&self: Iter(y, a)) -> y:
    if IterBlock.inWord(self.block) then return *(bitcast(addressOf(borrowMut(self.block).word)) :: %y)

    let &me = borrowMut(self.block)
    if exchange(me.slot, Nothing :: Maybe(y)) is Just(->value): return value
    return unreachableValue()

{-
   A value of any type, on a path that stops the program first.

   How "does not come back" is spelled in a function that must answer something: the language has no
   bottom type, so the path after `abortProcess` still needs an expression of the right type, and a
   call of this function is one whose type the position decides. It is never reached.
-}
fn (a) unreachableValue() -> a:
    abortProcess()
    return unreachableValue()

{-
   §2.4's manual step - `Iter.advance` and `Iter.take` as one answer, spelled `handle.next()`.
-}
pub fn (y, a) Iter.next(&self: Iter(y, a)) -> Maybe(y):
    if !Iter.advance(self) then return Nothing
    return Just(Iter.take(self))

{-
   The arguments, taken by the entry before it calls the producer.

   Taken rather than borrowed: the producer owns what it was given with `->`, and a tuple left behind
   would be dropped a second time by the handle's teardown.
-}
@builtin(iterBegin)
pub fn (y, a) IterBlock.begin(block: %IterBlock(y, {})) -> a:
    let own = bitcast(block) :: %IterBlock(y, a)
    let &me = borrowMut(own)

    if exchange(me.captured, Nothing :: Maybe(a)) is Just(->captured): return captured
    return unreachableValue()

-- `yield value`, from inside the producer. `True` where the consumer answered by cancelling.
@builtin(iterYield)
pub fn (y) IterBlock.handOver(block: %IterBlock(y, {}), ->value: y) -> Bool:
    -- The word is packed before the block is borrowed: a live borrow through a raw pointer may be
    -- pointing at any local whose address was taken, and `word`'s is.
    if IterBlock.inWord(block):
        let &word = 0 :: Size
        store(bitcast(addressOf(word)) :: %y, value)
        return suspendOn(addressOf(borrowMut(block).fiber), word) == kIterCancel

    let &me = borrowMut(block)
    me.slot = Just(value)
    return suspendOn(addressOf(me.fiber), kIterYielded) == kIterCancel

-- The end of the producer, which does not come back - see `Fiber.finishOn`.
@builtin(iterFinish)
pub fn (y) IterBlock.finish(block: %IterBlock(y, {})):
    let &me = borrowMut(block)
    finishOn(addressOf(me.fiber), kIterFinished)

{-
   The handle's teardown: cancel, release, give the stack back.

   A producer is resumed with `kCancel` for as long as it keeps suspending - a teardown on the way out
   may itself reach a `yield`, whose answer is the same - and whatever it handed over that nobody
   took, and whatever it was given and never ran to take, is dropped here, in this frame.
-}
instance (y, a) Drop(Iter(y, a)):
    fn drop(->value: Iter(y, a)):
        IterBlock.close(value.block)

fn (y, a) IterBlock.close(block: %IterBlock(y, a)):
    if isNull(block) then return

    let &me = borrowMut(block)

    while me.fiber.status == FiberStatus.Suspended:
        resume(me.fiber, kIterCancel)

    me.slot = Nothing
    me.captured = Nothing

    unmapMemory(me.stack, me.stackBytes)
